Collaboration Reflection

In the current mini-project II, we have worked in a shared GitHub repository that is in the github along with the project and each of the code is connected to an R project present in RStudio on our laptops. We have used the commit functionality along with pull, and push in order to stay in sync and to further practice different GitHub tools, we have resolved a merge conflict in the README file, we then opened and closed an issue, and merged a branch with the help of pull request.

We had to meet in order to work through the different scraping and cleaning and in the same process we had to save the scraped data to CSV files and so we did not have to rerun the process of slow price scraping and therefore having everything in one repository which helped to see each other's changes. I liked that we could both work on the project and see each other's progress. One challenge in the due process was making sure that we had the latest version of the files at all times synced.

For the next time, we would be using branches and pull requests more often instead of pushing the code straight to main, and we would be writing much clearer commit messages. In some of our messages such as "more changes", we  did not say much about what changed. I think we could  also plan our tasks earlier and communicate more about the changes we make. Overall, working together helped us finish the project and get more comfortable using GitHub and RStudio.
